./.lattice/story/actives/B-137a/findings.json:10465:      "desc": "packages/main/src/core/provider-process-registry.ts:336-349 — releaseProviderProcessSlot now THROWS where main's releaseSlot swallowed errors, but all 6 production call sites invoke it from a bare finally/catch, so a release failure REPLACES the in-flight exception or turns a successful provider run into a hard error. Verified: (a) the new function has no try/catch and calls withProviderRegistryLock (provider-process-registry.ts:267-291), which calls acquireMkdirLock with deadlineMs 30_000; mkdir-lock.ts:306 throws `<label> lock timeout after <ms>ms` on deadline. (b) `git show main:packages/main/src/core/host-run.ts` releaseSlot (line 706) wrapped the entire body in `try { ... } catch { /* Best-effort release; pid-death / the orphan backstop reclaims a leak. */ }`. (c) The smoke deliberately asserts BOTH throw paths — provider-process-registry-smoke.ts:326-334 (injected unlink failure) and :361-370 (`/provider-process-registry lock timeout/`, 'registry-lock mutation failure must reach the supervising owner') — yet no caller supervises it. Call sites: codex-review-leaf.ts:650 and autopilot-executor.ts:413 (bare finally, comments at :647-649 and :410-412 still say 'best-effort (pid-death / the orphan backstop reclaims a leak)'), planned-steps-executor.ts:470 and lattice-dispatch.ts:2484,:2725 (bare finally via releaseProviderProcessAdmission), host-run.ts:1862,:2021 (inside a catch that rethrows). Failure scenario: runCodexReviewLeaf's runner completes successfully and assigns `outcome`; control enters the finally at :648; a concurrent lattice-dispatch/autopilot process holds <slots>.lock for >30s, so acquireMkdirLock throws; per JS semantics a throw from finally discards the completed try block, so runCodexReviewLeaf propagates 'provider-process-registry lock timeout' and the cross-host review verdict that actually ran is reported as a hard error. Same interleaving with a failing runner (e.g. codex ENOENT) replaces the real ENOENT diagnostic with the lock-timeout message. Fix: wrap each release in try/catch (restoring main's best-effort semantics) or make the release failure an explicitly recorded diagnostic that never displaces the primary outcome; and correct the three stale 'best-effort' comments either way.",
